    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1850">

                <pb facs="1215" n="1099" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg1099"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 831.404/9.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Caffery to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 99.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1911-10-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 26, 1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose herewith
                    duplicate copies,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1850-f1"/> together with translations
                    thereof, of a decree of the President of Venezuela, extending the provisions of
                    the law of ecclesiastical patronage to foreign missionary societies.</p>
                <p>The Minister of the Interior, in a letter to the Presidents of the States,
                    Governor of the Federal District and Governors of the Territories, orders
                    compliance with this decree. He directs them to exercise all diligence in the
                    enforcement of the Law of Ecclesiastical Patronage, and in particular to the
                    dispositions of that Law and of the Constitution of the Republic, which reserve
                    to the National Congress the right to establish missions; to the Executive that
                    of regulating them, of engaging missionaries and sending them to the points
                    where there are natives to be civilized; and to the Chief Magistrate that of
                    prohibiting the entry of strangers consecrated especially to the service of one
                    cult.</p>
                <p>This decree, together with its interpretation by the Minister of the Interior,
                    subjects foreign missionary societies, with establishments in Venezuela, to
                    direct supervision by the Government, which thus acquires the right of
                    intermeddling in their affairs.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jefferson Caffery</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
